pinned to `claude.TestedVersion` with per-architecture checksums, git, make, the Go toolchain and the starter roles' tools at the scanner Dockerfiles' pins (jq, yq, osv-scanner, govulncheck, golangci-lint) plus shellcheck; no `gh`, no `ard` (the `pre` container copies a static one into `/ard-bin`), no credential helper, no AGPL tools (no trufflehog). It is built on the pinned `GO_IMAGE` like the `Dockerfile`, with no `RUN` on the target platform, and runs as 65532:65532 (`jobpod.AgentUID`) on a read-only root with `DISABLE_AUTOUPDATER=1`. It is **public** (owner decision on #320, which also covers redistributing the `claude` binary): `ard-build` builds it for `linux/amd64` and `linux/arm64` from the same commit as the ard image and pushes `ghcr.io/albertcbrown/ard/agent` by digest. Because both images are public they must hold no secrets: `make image-test` builds both and scans each exported filesystem (`internal/imagescan`, run by `deploy/imagescan`), failing on any credential-shaped file name (SSH keys, `.netrc`, `.git-credentials`, docker, kube, gh or claude credentials) or content (the redact package's token rules and PEM private keys with a body); the only exemptions are public test fixtures (Go's own testdata, GnuTLS self-test vectors), and a canary image with a planted credential must fail the same scan. Locally, `make agent-image` builds this host's platform as `ard-agent:dev`. `TestAgentImagePins` keeps the claude version and checksums in step with `TestedVersion`, and `TestAgentImageToolPins` keeps the tool pins equal to the scanners'.\n - **Image pins for the Kubernetes executor.** The deploy job pins the agent image's digest beside the ard image's in the deployment repository: `deploy/argocd` takes `agentImage: {repository, digest}` and passes it to each child, and with `executor.kind: k8s` `deploy/charts/ard-serve` renders `ARD_K8S_AGENT_IMAGE=<repository>@<digest>` and `ARD_K8S_POD_IMAGE=<ard image>@<digest>` (`pre` and `post` run the ard image). Both values are digest placeholders (`digest: \"\"`) in this repository; with executor k8s an empty or malformed agent digest, or a tag in the repository, is refused, and executor local renders no `ARD_K8S_*` env. To fill them, the deploy job reads each pushed digest (`docker buildx imagetools inspect ghcr.io/albertcbrown/ard/agent:sha-<7> --format '{{json .Manifest.Digest}}'`), verifies the signature and revision label as for the ard image, and writes `agentImage.digest` in the same commit as `image.digest`. No `imagePullSecrets`: both images are public.\n-- **Role images ([#245](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/245)).** Owner decisions: role `Dockerfile`s are built **in the cluster** when a job needs one, not in CI; the images are **public** on ghcr as `<roleImages.repository>/<type>-<name>` (default `ghcr.io/albertcbrown/ard/<type>-<name>`), so job pods pull them with no `imagePullSecrets`; a role image built on the agent image starts `FROM ghcr.io/albertcbrown/ard/agent:latest`, so `ard-build` must also move the agent image's `:latest` tag. The chart side (#448, off by default): with `roleImages.enabled` and executor k8s, `deploy/charts/ard-serve` creates the build namespace `ard-build-<instance>`, apart from the jobs namespace because builder pods hold the push credential. Rootless BuildKit (`roleImages.builder.image`, pinned by digest) needs seccomp and AppArmor Unconfined, which Pod Security restricted and baseline forbid, so that namespace enforces privileged and warns and audits at baseline; a ValidatingAdmissionPolicy admits only the builder shape instead: the `ard-build` ServiceAccount with no token, exactly one `buildkit` container running that exact image as `roleImages.builder.uid`, not privileged and adding no capabilities, no host namespaces, image pull secrets or envFrom, and Secret volumes limited to the push credential and the pod's own Secret. Builder pods reach DNS and the internet minus the private ranges, and nothing reaches them. The push credential is a `kubernetes.io/dockerconfigjson` Secret, optionally built by an ExternalSecret from one item's `username` and `token` (a classic PAT with `write:packages`; ghcr refuses App and fine-grained tokens). The controller gets the build namespace, builder image and uid, role image repository and the push Secret's name as `ARD_K8S_BUILD_NAMESPACE`, `ARD_K8S_BUILDER_IMAGE`, `ARD_K8S_BUILDER_UID`, `ARD_K8S_ROLE_IMAGE_REPOSITORY` and `ARD_K8S_ROLE_IMAGE_PUSH_SECRET`, never the credential, and a Role in the build namespace like the jobs one. **Residual risks:** the controller's token can create builder pods that mount the push credential, so a stolen controller token can push to the role image packages (the admission policy limits it to the pinned BuildKit image, not to a given build); and with a `:latest` base, a role image tagged by its role directory keeps an older agent base until the role changes. Each package must be made public once after its first push, and AGPL roles (`trufflehog`) stay off under k8s so they are never published. Runbook: [docs/runbooks/role-images.md](runbooks/role-images.md).\n+- **Role images ([#245](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/245)).** Owner decisions: role `Dockerfile`s are built **in the cluster** when a job needs one, not in CI; the images are **public** on ghcr as `<roleImages.repository>/<type>-<name>` (default `ghcr.io/albertcbrown/ard/<type>-<name>`), so job pods pull them with no `imagePullSecrets`; a role image built on the agent image starts `FROM ghcr.io/albertcbrown/ard/agent:latest`, so `ard-build` must also move the agent image's `:latest` tag. The chart side (#448, off by default): with `roleImages.enabled` and executor k8s, `deploy/charts/ard-serve` creates the build namespace `ard-build-<instance>`, apart from the jobs namespace because builder pods hold the push credential. Rootless BuildKit (`roleImages.builder.image`, pinned by digest) needs seccomp and AppArmor Unconfined, which Pod Security restricted and baseline forbid, so that namespace enforces privileged and warns and audits at baseline; a ValidatingAdmissionPolicy admits only the builder shape instead: the `ard-build` ServiceAccount with no token, exactly one `buildkit` container running that exact image as `roleImages.builder.uid`, not privileged and adding no capabilities, no host namespaces, image pull secrets or envFrom, and Secret volumes limited to the push credential and the pod's own Secret. Builder pods reach DNS and the internet minus the private ranges, and nothing reaches them. The push credential is a `kubernetes.io/dockerconfigjson` Secret, optionally built by an ExternalSecret from one item's `username` and `token` (a classic PAT with `write:packages`; ghcr refuses App and fine-grained tokens). The controller gets the build namespace, builder image and uid, role image repository and the push Secret's name as `ARD_K8S_BUILD_NAMESPACE`, `ARD_K8S_BUILDER_IMAGE`, `ARD_K8S_BUILDER_UID`, `ARD_K8S_ROLE_IMAGE_REPOSITORY` and `ARD_K8S_ROLE_IMAGE_PUSH_SECRET`, never the credential, and a Role in the build namespace like the jobs one. **Residual risks:** the controller's token can create builder pods that mount the push credential, so a stolen controller token can push to the role image packages (the admission policy limits it to the pinned BuildKit image, not to a given build); and with a `:latest` base, a role image tagged by its role directory keeps an older agent base until the role changes. Each package must be made public once after its first push, and AGPL roles (`trufflehog`) stay off under k8s so they are never published. **Build (#449):** with the build settings present, `Prepare` of a job whose role has a `Dockerfile` builds the image before the worktree exists, so a failed build leaves nothing behind. The tag is `h-<32 hex of a sha256>` over the sorted mode, path and blob id of every file in the role directory **at the pinned role SHA** (read with `git ls-tree` in the controller clone) plus the base image, so an unchanged role is built once. The controller checks the registry first (anonymous token, then `HEAD` of the manifest; 401, 403 and 404 mean not built, since a still-private package looks like a missing one; any other error fails the job as a registry check failure). On a miss it exports the directory with `git archive` at that SHA (symlinks, submodules, a missing `Dockerfile` and more than 512 KiB are refused), stores it in a Secret named after the builder pod, and creates one builder pod in the build namespace (`ard-build-<tag>`, the shape the admission policy admits, restart never, `activeDeadlineSeconds` and the controller's wait both bound by the build budget, default 15 minutes). The pod runs `buildctl` with `AGENT_IMAGE` set and writes the pushed digest to its termination message. Concurrent jobs for one role share one build. A failed build (exit code and the redacted last log lines), a timeout, a refused pod or a missing digest fails the job with that cause; the pod and Secret are always deleted. The job's agent container then runs the image **by digest** (the pre and post containers keep the pod image). Only the controller requests builds, and the build context is the human-owned role directory at the pinned SHA, never a PR worktree. Runbook: [docs/runbooks/role-images.md](runbooks/role-images.md).\n - **Deploy.** A deploy job verifies the signature and the revision label, checks the new commit descends from the pinned one, and pins the digest and commit in the deployment repository. Argo CD renders this repository's `deploy/argocd` chart (the app of apps) with that pin and the site's values: one child Application per target repository, named `ard-<instance>`, reading `deploy/charts/ard-serve` at the pinned commit, so chart and image move together. The cluster admits no other Application from that chart.\n - **Versioning ([#183](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/183)).** `version.yml` gives every first-parent merge to `main` an annotated tag `vX.Y.Z` and a notes-only release, oldest first. The bump comes from the merged PR's labels: `semver:major`, `semver:minor`, else patch; a direct push gets patch, and ard's own main-lane commits (committer `ARD_BOT_LOGIN`) are skipped. The seed tag is `v0.1.0`. The workflow is a dry run until the repository variable `ARD_VERSIONING` is `on`. `make build` (`git describe` limited to plain `vX.Y.Z` tags) and `go install ...@vX.Y.Z` (build info) both print the tag from `ard version`. `internal/versioncheck` tests the workflow's scripts. See `docs/runbooks/release.md`.\n - **Homebrew ([#177](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/177)).** `.github/workflows/release.yml` builds each new `v*` tag with GoReleaser (`.goreleaser.yaml`: darwin and linux, arm64 and amd64, CGO off, `-X main.version`), adds the archives and `checksums.txt` to the release `version.yml` created (`release.mode: keep-existing`) and updates the cask in `albertcbrown/homebrew-tap` with `TAP_GITHUB_TOKEN`, a token scoped to the tap and held only in the `release` environment. It stays inert until `ARD_VERSIONING` is `on` and that token exists, and on a tag push it releases only the newest tag, so a backfill publishes once. Gatekeeper: the binary is not yet signed or notarized, so the cask clears the quarantine flag after install (`hooks.post.install`), an accepted risk; notarization is a follow-up.\ndiff --git a/docs/runbooks/role-images.md b/docs/runbooks/role-images.md\nindex 79d7e91..423dbb7 100644\n--- a/docs/runbooks/role-images.md\n+++ b/docs/runbooks/role-images.md\n@@ -7,8 +7,10 @@ plain agent image. See\n [docs/proposal.md: Executors and the Reaper](../proposal.md#executors-and-the-reaper)\n and [Delivery](../proposal.md#delivery).\n \n-The chart side is #448. ard serve uses it once #449 lands; until then the chart\n-objects exist and nothing creates builder pods.\n+The chart side is #448; ard serve (#449) uses it: when a job's role has a\n+`Dockerfile`, it checks the re